{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"agent_message","text":"## Findings

### High — Handoff tokens authorize normal product requests

`authenticate()` accepts either a `product_session` cookie or a `handoff` query parameter and returns the token’s account without checking its audience or purpose (`auth.py:2-4`).

A valid token issued as:

- `audience="provider-browser"`
- `purpose="complete-link"`

therefore authenticates as an ordinary product user. This violates the contract and allows anyone holding a short-lived handoff token to access normal product endpoints.

The same issue applies if a handoff token is placed in the `product_session` cookie.

### High — Handoff tokens are replayable

The nonce and single-use requirement are never validated or consumed. The same signed handoff token can be submitted repeatedly until expiry. A synthetic check confirmed two successful authentications using the same token.

`provider_return()` issues the handoff token, but no corresponding one-time redemption path exists (`auth.py:7-10`).

### Medium — Credentials in query strings can leak

Using `request.query.get("handoff")` places a bearer credential in URLs. It may be retained in browser history, reverse-proxy/application logs, analytics, bookmarks, and referrer headers. Anyone obtaining such a URL can use the token during its validity window.

## Recommended fix

- Product authentication should accept only a token with `audience="product"` from the product-session mechanism.
- Handoff tokens should be accepted only by a dedicated account-link callback.
- That callback must validate audience, purpose, expiry, account, and nonce, then atomically mark the nonce/token as consumed.
- Do not use handoff tokens as the ordinary browser session; issue a separate product session after successful linking.
- Avoid query-string bearer tokens where possible; if unavoidable, redeem immediately and redirect to a cleaned URL."}}
